egro","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Montenegro"},{"qid":"Q37024","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Flag of Serbia and Montenegro (1992–2006).svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q37024","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of Serbia and Montenegro.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q37024","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q33031","value_label":"Vojislav Koštunica","from_t":2000.7650273224044,"to_t":2003.1780821917807,"title":"President of Serbia and Montenegro","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Vojislav Koštunica"},{"qid":"Q37024","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q431413","value_label":"Svetozar Marović","from_t":2003.1780821917807,"to_t":2006.4191780821918,"title":"President of Serbia and Montenegro","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Svetozar Marović"},{"qid":"Q37024","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q719140","value_label":"Flag of Serbia and Montenegro (1992–2006).svg","from_t":1992.3196721311476,"to_t":2006,"title":"flag of Serbia and Montenegro","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Serbia and Montenegro"}],"people":{"Q207384":{"label":"Alexander I of Yugoslavia","description":"Prince regent of Kingdom of Serbia and later King of Yugoslavia 1921–34","enwiki":"Alexander I of Yugoslavia","summary":"Alexander I Karađorđević (Serbo-Croatian: Александар I Карађорђевић, Aleksandar I Karađorđević, 16 December [O.S. 4 December] 1888 – 9 October 1934) was King of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes from 16 August 1921 to 3 October 1929 and King of Yugoslavia from 3 October 1929 until his assassination. His thirteen-year reign was the longest of the kingdom's three monarchs. He served as the de facto head of state of the Kingdom of Serbia from 1914 to 1918 and of Yugoslavia from 1918 to 1921 as prince regent for his father, Peter I.\nBorn in Cetinje, Montenegro, Alexander was the second son and fourth child of Peter and Zorka Karađorđević. The House of Karađorđević had been deposed in Serbia 30 years earlier by the rival House of Obrenović, and Alexander spent his early life in exile with his father in Montenegro and later Switzerland.","image":"Propriété d' Albert Kahn , Boulogne , France S .A .R Alexandre , Prince Régent du Royaume des Serbes , Croates et Slovènes.jpg","birth":1888.9562841530055,"death":1934.7698630136986},"Q223183":{"label":"Peter II of Yugoslavia","description":"last King of Yugoslavia from 1934 to 1945 (1923-1970)","enwiki":"Peter II of Yugoslavia","summary":"Peter II Karađorđević (Serbo-Croatian: Петар II Карађорђевић, Petar II Karađorđević; 6 September 1923 – 3 November 1970) was the last King of Yugoslavia, reigning from October 1934 until he was deposed in November 1945. He was the last reigning member of the Karađorđević dynasty.\nThe eldest child of King Alexander I and Maria of Romania, Peter acceded to the Yugoslav throne in 1934 at the age of 11 after his father was assassinated during a state visit to Marseille. A regency was set up under his cousin Prince Paul.","image":"Kralj Petar II.jpg","birth":1923.6794520547944,"death":1970.8383561643836},"Q369612":{"label":"Dobrica Ćosić","description":"Serbian politician and writer (1921-2014)","enwiki":"Dobrica Ćosić","summary":"Dobrica Ćosić (Serbian Cyrillic: Добрица Ћосић, pronounced [dǒbritsa tɕôːsitɕ]; 29 December 1921 – 18 May 2014) was a Yugoslav and Serbian writer, politician and political theorist.\nĆosić was twice awarded the prestigious NIN award for literature and Medal of Pushkin for his writing. His books have been translated into 30 languages.\nHe was the first President of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia with his tenure lasting from 1992 to 1993.","image":"S.Kragujevic, Dobrica Cosic 1961.JPG","birth":1921.9917808219177,"death":2014.3753424657534},"Q9161":{"label":"Josip Broz Tito","description":"Yugoslav revolutionary and statesman (1892−1980)","enwiki":"Josip Broz Tito","summary":"Josip Broz (7 May 1892 – 4 May 1980), commonly known as Tito, was a Yugoslav communist revolutionary and politician who led Yugoslavia as prime minister from 1943 to 1963 and as president from 1953 until his death in 1980. He was the longtime leader of the League of Communists of Yugoslavia, supreme commander of the Yugoslav Partisans during World War II, and one of the founders of the Non-Aligned Movement. The political ideology and policies associated with his rule are known as Titoism.\nTito was born in Kumrovec in present-day Croatia, then part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.","image":"Josip Broz Tito uniform portrait.jpg","birth":1892.3469945355191,"death":1980.3387978142077},"Q47139":{"label":"Slobodan Milošević","description":"Yugoslav–Serbian politician (1941–2006)","enwiki":"Slobodan Milošević","summary":"Slobodan Milošević (Serbian Cyrillic: Слободан Милошевић, pronounced [slobǒdan milǒːʃevitɕ] , 20 August 1941 – 11 March 2006) was a Yugoslav and Serbian politician who was the president of Serbia between 1989 and 1997 and president of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia from 1997 until his overthrow in 2000. Milošević played a major role in the Yugoslav Wars and became the first sitting head of state charged with war crimes, as well as the first former head of state charged with crimes against humanity and genocide.\nBorn in Požarevac, he studied law at the University of Belgrade Faculty of Law during which he joined the League of Socialist Youth of Yugoslavia. From the 1960s, he was advisor to the mayor of Belgrade, and in the 1970s he was a chairman of large companies as the protégé of Serbian leader Ivan Stambolić.","image":"Stevan Kragujević, Slobodan Milošević, portret.jpg","birth":1941.6328767123289,"death":2006.1890410958904},"Q33031":{"label":"Vojislav Koštunica","description":"Serbian politician","enwiki":"Vojislav Koštunica","summary":"Vojislav Koštunica (born 24 March 1944) is a Serbian former politician who served as the last president of FR Yugoslavia from 2000 to 2003 and as the prime minister of Serbia from 2004 to 2008.\nKoštunica won the 2000 Yugoslav presidential election as a candidate of a broad alliance Democratic Opposition of Serbia (DOS), which led to the overthrow of Slobodan Milošević and the withdrawal of international sanctions against Yugoslavia. He strictly opposed cooperation with the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY) and his party left the coalition government in protest at the decision to extradite Slobodan Milošević to the ICTY. After the 2003 Serbian parliamentary election, the first elections after the dissolution of DOS and assassination of Prime Minister Zoran Đinđić, Koštunica formed a minority government with the support of Milošević's Socialist Party of Serbia and became the head of government. He was one of the crucial figures in the adoption of the first constitution of an independent Serbia, as well as for declaring Serbia a neutral country.","image":"Vojislav Koštunica (2009).jpg","birth":1944.2267759562842,"death":null},"Q893817":{"label":"Borisav Jović","description":"Serbian diplomat (1928–2021)","enwiki":"Borisav Jović","summary":"Borisav Jović (Serbian Cyrillic: Борисав Јовић, pronounced [jǒːʋit͡ɕ]; 19 October 1928 – 13 September 2021) was a Serbian economist, diplomat and politician who served as the President of the Presidency of Yugoslavia from 1990 to 1991.\nHe was Yugoslavia's ambassador to Italy from the mid to late 1970s, was the Serbian representative of the collective Presidency of Yugoslavia during the late 1980s and early 1990s, was the President of the Presidency and was a leading figure in the Socialist Party of Serbia in the 1990s, playing a key role during the Breakup of Yugoslavia and the Yugoslav Wars. Jović received his PhD in economics from the University of Belgrade in 1965. He was a fluent speaker of Russian and Italian.","image":"Borisav Jović.jpg","birth":1928.7978142076502,"death":2021.6986301369864},"Q220533":{"label":"Zoran Lilić","description":"Serbian politician","enwiki":"Zoran Lilić","summary":"Zoran Lilić (Serbian Cyrillic: Зоран Лилић; born 27 August 1953) is a Serbian and former Yugoslav politician. He served as President of the National Assembly of Serbia in 1993, and as President of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia from 1993 to 1997.","image":null,"birth":1953.6520547945206,"death":null},"Q431413":{"label":"Svetozar Marović","description":"Montenegrin politician","enwiki":"Svetozar Marović","summary":"Svetozar Marović (Montenegrin Cyrillic: Светозар Маровић; born 31 March 1955) is a Montenegrin lawyer and politician who served as the last head of state and head of government of Serbia and Montenegro from 2003 until Montenegro's declaration of independence in 2006.\nOn 15 December 2015, he was arrested in Montenegro for charges of corruption, and on 18 August 2017, his family's assets were frozen. He currently resides in Serbia, where he fled, just before he was found guilty in 2017. Montenegro has repeatedly requested his extradition from Serbia.","image":"Svetozar Marović crop.jpg","birth":1955.2438356164384,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Yugoslavia (1918–1941).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flag_of_Yugoslavia_%281918%E2%80%931941%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Yugoslavia_%281918%E2%80%931941%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yugoslavia_(1918%E2%80%931941).svg","author":"Fibonacci","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of the Kingdom of Yugoslavia.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Yugoslavia.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Yugoslavia.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Yugoslavia.svg","author":"R-41","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Yugoslavia (1946-1992).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Flag_of_Yugoslavia_%281946-1992%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Yugoslavia_%281946-1992%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yugoslavia_(1946-1992).svg","author":"Flag designed by Đorđe Andrejević-Kun[3]SVG coding: Zscout370","license":"Public domain"},"Emblem of Yugoslavia (1963–1992).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Emblem_of_Yugoslavia_%281963%E2%80%931992%29.svg/330px-Emblem_of_Yugoslavia_%281963%E2%80%931992%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Yugoslavia_(1963%E2%80%931992).svg","author":"Đorđe Andrejević-Kun","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Serbia and Montenegro (1992–2006).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Flag_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro_%281992%E2%80%932006%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro_%281992%E2%80%932006%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro_(1992%E2%80%932006).svg","author":"See File history below for details. (www.gov.yu)","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Serbia and Montenegro.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Coat_of_arms_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro.svg","author":null,"license":"Public domain"},"Propriété d' Albert Kahn , Boulogne , France S .A .R Alexandre , Prince